window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-7544d5535380cda3"]={"key":"ny_main-7544d5535380cda3","group":"ny_main","uid":"7544d5535380cda3","rank":34,"title":"authored doc policy impact england scotland 001","occupation":"news_analysts_reporters_journalists","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are Selma Novotny, the news analyst at Ambergate Services, preparing a revision for Oona Bruns before Ibrahim Dunbar and Jacinta Yamamoto take the material to the operating committee. Genevieve Mbeki has challenged the draft forecast for the proposed customer-notification rules affecting customers of Overbrook Laboratories and Duncastle Mutual in England and Scotland, and Eamon Dalgaard and Eamon Ilechukwu need a version that frontline colleagues can use during next month’s rollout. The source bundle is not available in this handoff, so treat the following as the complete frozen record as at 30 September 2025 and do not add outside figures or invented citations: the complaint logs show median time from the first customer contact to the recorded outcome moving from 18 hours to 16 hours in England between January–June 2024 and January–June 2025, while Scotland moves from 20 hours to 24 hours; the internal draft says a single 12-hour response-time rule would produce 90% compliance in both places with staffing unchanged; one policy extract calls the measure “acknowledgement,” another calls it “response,” the England log records first customer contact, and the Scotland log records closure, while an interview note says different teams use “response time” for both first acknowledgement and full resolution. Managers have already been repeating the 12-hour forecast as though it were a statutory deadline. Prepare one revised audience-specific policy-impact report that reconciles this record, compares the two periods, projects outcomes under the draft-as-written and a clearly labelled evidence-based comparison scenario, identifies where the one-clear-rule-everywhere assumption breaks down, and marks the boundary between journalistic analysis and legal or compliance advice. Make the assumptions and unknowns visible, keep England and Scotland distinct, actively discourage the 12-hour misuse, and pin down the meaning of “response time” before relying on it. Give the committee exactly one evidence-based recommendation on whether to adopt the draft communication as written; do not offer several options or end with a list of next steps. Include a concise frontline-ready communication box, keep the report to eight pages and 1,800 words, and write it so the named readers can use it without asking you what a figure or label means. Write the single file `revised_policy_impact_report.docx` into the top level of the working directory the solver starts in, using exactly that filename.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `revised_policy_impact_report.docx` — A revised audience-specific policy-impact report presentation for the operating committee and frontline communication leads. (Write to the top level of the solver's starting working directory.; Use exactly the specified filename.; Do not exceed eight pages or 1,800 words.; Use only quantitative inputs stated in the request, and label assumptions, projections, and unknowns.; End with one clear recommendation rather than a list of next steps.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/revised_policy_impact_report.docx","bytes":44370,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/22fe846238d8527df806f4a1d51ed3fe0a49dd04f44919f8b2ff8d995e268705.docx","sha256":"22fe846238d8527df806f4a1d51ed3fe0a49dd04f44919f8b2ff8d995e268705","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"DECISION BRIEF  |  PREPARED FOR THE OPERATING COMMITTEE\nRevised Policy-Impact Report\nThe 12-hour message is not ready for a single England-and-Scotland rollout\nPrepared by\nSelma Novotny, news analyst\nFor decision\nOona Bruns; operating committee via Ibrahim Dunbar and Jacinta Yamamoto\nFrontline use\nEamon Dalgaard and Eamon Ilechukwu; next month's rollout\nScope\nCustomer-notification rules for Overbrook Laboratories and Duncastle Mutual in England and Scotland\nRecord status\nFrozen factual record as at 30 September 2025; source bundle unavailable\n\nCommittee readout\nThe decision is whether to adopt the draft communication as written before next month's rollout. It is under review because Genevieve Mbeki challenged a precise forecast that sits beside opposite regional movements and an unresolved definition of the measured event.\n\nEngland: the observed median moved from 18 hours in January-June 2024 to 16 hours in January-June 2025, an improvement. Scotland: the observed median moved from 20 to 24 hours, a deterioration. The draft instead forecasts 90% compliance with a uniform 12-hour target in both territories, assuming staffing is unchanged. That forecast is not an observed result.\nRead this boundary before relying on the jurisdictional analysis\nThis is journalistic and editorial analysis for a communication decision, not legal or compliance advice. The source bundle was unavailable, so the factual record is limited to the information in the brief and is frozen as at 30 September 2025. The missing regulatory snapshot and policy extracts prevent a definitive legal conclusion; no statement here establishes a UK-wide requirement.\n\nWhat each audience needs\nOperating committee: a disciplined adoption decision, with the status of every figure visible. Frontline communication leads: plain wording that distinguishes acknowledgement from completion, keeps England and Scotland separate, and does not turn an internal forecast into a promise.\n\n\n01  /  METRIC RECONCILIATION\nDefine the event before comparing the hours\nWorking editorial definition: in this report, response time means the elapsed hours from a customer's first contact until the outcome is recorded or the case is closed. This is the principal comparison endpoint. It is a completion interval, not a first acknowledgement interval.\nAcknowledgement is the first confirmation that contact was received. Resolution or closure is completion of the case, or the recorded outcome. They are separate events. The interview note says teams use response time for both, so the same label can describe different clocks.\nWhy the figures are called response-time proxies\nOne policy extract says acknowledgement; another says response. The England log records first customer contact, while the Scotland log records closure or recorded outcome. The brief describes medians from first contact to recorded outcome, but the unavailable source bundle cannot confirm that the log fields are like-for-like. The table therefore reports the supplied observations as response-time proxies, not as a validated common metric.\n\nObserved complaint-log record\nTerritory\nLog convention in frozen record\nJanuary-June 2024\nJanuary-June 2025\nMovement\nEvidence status\nEngland\nFirst customer contact is named in the log; endpoint needs confirmation\n18 hours\nObserved median\n16 hours\nObserved median\n-2 hours\nImproving\nObserved; not causal\nScotland\nClosure / recorded outcome is named in the log\n20 hours\nObserved median\n24 hours\nObserved median\n+4 hours\nDeteriorating\nObserved; not causal\n\nThe directions are opposite: England improves from 18 to 16 hours, while Scotland worsens from 20 to 24 hours. The movement is an observation across two periods, not proof that the proposed rules caused either change. The frozen record supplies no basis to attribute Scotland's deterioration to staffing, process, law, or any other cause.\nA combined average would hide this split and is not used. England and Scotland remain separate analytical units wherever their underlying log conventions differ.\n\n\n02  /  SCENARIO COMPARISON\nSeparate the draft forecast from an evidence-based baseline\nThe draft-as-written scenario is a forecast supplied by the internal draft. The comparison scenario is a transparent conditional projection from the latest observed medians. Neither scenario repairs the missing metric definition; their different evidence status must remain visible.\nDimension\nA. Draft-as-written\nForecast\nB. Evidence-based comparison\nConditional projection\nTarget\nUniform 12-hour target\nMetric meaning unspecified\nNo new target asserted\nUse the defined response-time proxy\nEngland\n90% compliance forecast\nStaffing unchanged\n16-hour latest observed median used as a conditional baseline\n12-hour compliance percentage unknown\nScotland\n90% compliance forecast\nStaffing unchanged\n24-hour latest observed median used as a conditional baseline\n12-hour compliance percentage unknown\nStatus\nDraft forecast for both territories; not an observation and not a statute\nEvidence-based comparison projection; not a compliance forecast\n\nHow to read the comparison\nUnder the draft's 12-hour target, the latest observed median sits 4 hours above target in England (16 minus 12) and 12 hours above target in Scotland (24 minus 12). These are median gaps, not compliance rates. The brief provides no distribution, denominator, or confirmed common endpoint from which to derive a percentage.\nScenario B therefore projects only the central interval that would be seen if near-term conditions resembled January-June 2025: 16 hours in England and 24 hours in Scotland. It does not claim that either median will persist, and it leaves the 12-hour compliance percentage unknown. This is the most that the supplied observations can support without inventing operational data.\nAssumptions made visible\nScenario A assumes one endpoint, one rule, both territories, and unchanged staffing. Scenario B assumes the latest regional medians are usable as comparison baselines only; it assumes no causal effect, no staffing change, and no unprovided compliance percentage. Both assumptions remain provisional.\n\nGenevieve Mbeki's challenge is therefore an evidentiary concern, not a disagreement over presentation style: the draft gives a single precise forecast where the record shows divergent movement and an unresolved clock.\n\n\n03  /  INTERPRETATION AND LIMITS\nWhere the one-clear-rule-everywhere assumption breaks down\nThe observed England-Scotland difference may partly be terminology or measurement: the extracts use acknowledgement and response inconsistently, the logs name different events, and teams use response time for both first acknowledgement and full resolution. It may also reflect a genuine difference in applicable requirements. The frozen record does not establish which explanation is correct.\nDo not infer that one England rule automatically applies across the whole of the UK. The unavailable regulatory snapshot and policy extracts mean this report cannot distinguish a genuine jurisdiction-sensitive requirement from an unresolved evidence gap. Every jurisdiction-sensitive statement here is bounded by the frozen record or marked for verification; no legal threshold has been invented.\nEvidence status: what is known and what remains open\nStatus\nStatement in this report\nObserved facts\nThe four regional medians and their movements; the labels and log conventions described in the brief.\nDraft forecast\nA 12-hour uniform target and 90% compliance forecast for England and Scotland.\nAssumption\nThe draft keeps staffing unchanged; Scenario B uses the latest regional median as a comparison proxy only.\nEditorial interpretation\nOpposite regional movement plus a disputed endpoint makes the uniform message unsafe to adopt as written.\nUnknowns\nThe legal or regulatory requirement, the common endpoint, the denominator behind the forecast, the cause of Scotland's movement, and whether the logs are comparable.\n\nStop the 12-hour misuse\nManagers have been repeating the 12-hour forecast as though it were a statutory deadline. Do not repeat or present it that way. It is the draft's internal target and forecast under unchanged staffing; that label alone does not establish a legal deadline, and meeting it would not prove regulatory compliance.\n\nThis report can assess whether the communication is editorially ready. It cannot resolve the legal or compliance status of the proposed rule, and it does not treat the absence of a supplied regulatory source as evidence that no requirement exists.\n\n\n04  /  FRONTLINE WORDING\nA safe message for next month's rollout\nFrontline-ready communication | use without translating the analyst's terms\nFor customers of Overbrook Laboratories and Duncastle Mutual, use the territory-specific rollout wording. In this report, response time means the hours from a customer's first contact until the outcome is recorded or the case is closed. An acknowledgement is only the first confirmation that contact was received; it is not the same as resolution. The draft's 12-hour figure is an internal planning target and forecast, not a statutory deadline. Do not describe it as a legal deadline, promise 12 hours, or merge England and Scotland into one unqualified rule. Questions about legal requirements need the approved jurisdiction-specific answer; this report is editorial analysis, not legal or compliance advice.\n\nDecision test\nA communication adopted as written would transmit a uniform 12-hour and 90% claim even though England and Scotland move in opposite directions, the logs do not establish a common response-time endpoint, and the percentage is a forecast rather than observed compliance. It would also leave the existing statutory-deadline misuse intact. The frontline wording above keeps the metric, territory, and legal boundary explicit.\nRecommendation\nDo not adopt the draft communication as written. Revise it before adoption because England's observed median improved from 18 to 16 hours while Scotland's worsened from 20 to 24 hours, the logs do not establish a common response-time endpoint, and the 90% figure is a forecast under unchanged staffing rather than evidence of a statutory deadline. This is an editorial communication decision; it does not determine legal or compliance status.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A file named revised_policy_impact_report.docx is present at the top level of the submitted working directory.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Submitted file path and filename.","grader_hint":"Inspect the top-level directory and confirm the exact filename and DOCX extension."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"execution","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The DOCX opens successfully and its text and tables are extractable without corruption.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Open/render test of the submitted DOCX.","grader_hint":"Open with a standard offline office library or renderer; award only if the document is materially readable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered report occupies no more than eight pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX to PDF or inspect pagination with an office renderer; count pages, not section breaks."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report contains no more than 1,800 words, excluding the filename and document metadata.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Word count from the submitted document.","grader_hint":"Count body text, table text, captions, and callout text using an offline parser; do not count headers or footers twice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document clearly identifies itself as a policy-impact report or equivalent revised policy-impact communication.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Title or opening text.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent professional wording, not a generic title such as 'Report' alone."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report identifies the operating committee as its primary decision audience.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience statement or executive framing.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit reference to the operating committee, not merely 'stakeholders.'"},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report distinguishes the committee audience from the frontline communication audience and explains what each needs from the report.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience framing and frontline section.","grader_hint":"Confirm that governance/decision use and frontline-use implications are not collapsed into one undifferentiated audience."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The scope names England and Scotland and relates the analysis to customer-notification rules.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope statement.","grader_hint":"Both territories and the notification-policy subject must be explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report states that the analysis is relevant to the rollout planned for the following month or otherwise preserves that timing context.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timing reference.","grader_hint":"Accept 'next month's rollout' or an equivalent reference tied to the brief."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The opening gives the committee a concise account of what decision the report supports and why the draft is under review.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening summary.","grader_hint":"Judge whether a committee reader can understand the purpose and decision context without reading the entire document."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report discloses that the source bundle was unavailable and that the factual record is limited to the information in the brief.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitations or methodology note.","grader_hint":"The limitation must be visible, not inferred from missing citations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report labels the record as frozen or current only as at 30 September 2025, or uses an equivalent explicit as-of date.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"As-of statement.","grader_hint":"Require the date or an unambiguous equivalent such as 'record frozen 30 September 2025.'"},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The first comparison period is identified as January–June 2024 or an unambiguous equivalent.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period labels.","grader_hint":"Inspect narrative, tables, and charts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The second comparison period is identified as January–June 2025 or an unambiguous equivalent.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period labels.","grader_hint":"Inspect narrative, tables, and charts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The England median for January–June 2024 is reported as 18 hours (±0.1 hours).","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"England period-comparison table or text.","grader_hint":"Locate the England 2024 value and allow only rounding within ±0.1 hours."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The England median for January–June 2025 is reported as 16 hours (±0.1 hours).","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"England period-comparison table or text.","grader_hint":"Locate the England 2025 value and allow only rounding within ±0.1 hours."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Scotland median for January–June 2024 is reported as 20 hours (±0.1 hours).","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scotland period-comparison table or text.","grader_hint":"Locate the Scotland 2024 value and allow only rounding within ±0.1 hours."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Scotland median for January–June 2025 is reported as 24 hours (±0.1 hours).","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scotland period-comparison table or text.","grader_hint":"Locate the Scotland 2025 value and allow only rounding within ±0.1 hours."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report correctly characterizes England as improving from 18 to 16 hours rather than deteriorating.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Interpretation of the England comparison.","grader_hint":"Check both the direction and the numerical basis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report correctly characterizes Scotland as deteriorating from 20 to 24 hours rather than improving.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Interpretation of the Scotland comparison.","grader_hint":"Check both the direction and the numerical basis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The report keeps the England and Scotland trends visible instead of using an unqualified combined average that could conceal the divergent movements.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison presentation.","grader_hint":"A combined figure may appear only if the regional values remain prominent and separately interpretable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The draft's proposed uniform target is identified as 12 hours (±0.1 hours).","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Draft scenario description.","grader_hint":"Confirm that 12 hours is clearly tied to the draft proposal, not presented as an observed result."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The draft forecast of 90% compliance is reported as 90% (±0.5 percentage points) and tied to both England and Scotland.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Draft forecast table or text.","grader_hint":"The figure must be described as a forecast and must cover both territories."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The draft scenario states or preserves the assumption that staffing is unchanged.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scenario assumptions.","grader_hint":"Accept 'no additional staffing' or equivalent wording."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report presents a clearly labelled draft-as-written or uniform-rule scenario.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scenario headings or table labels.","grader_hint":"The reader must be able to distinguish this scenario from observed performance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report presents a second, clearly labelled evidence-based comparison scenario rather than presenting only the draft forecast.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scenario comparison.","grader_hint":"Require a distinct second scenario with its own label and assumptions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison scenario uses the observed regional medians as its explicit evidence base or explains another defensible use of the four observed values.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scenario assumptions and calculations.","grader_hint":"Check that the second scenario is anchored to the period data and is not an unsupported invented forecast."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The two scenarios are shown side by side or otherwise in a form that lets a reader compare the target, geography, and status of each figure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scenario table or equivalent comparison.","grader_hint":"A reader should not have to reconstruct the comparison from scattered paragraphs."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every scenario figure is labelled as observed, forecast, target, assumption, or projection rather than presented as an undifferentiated fact.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Labels, captions, and nearby explanatory text.","grader_hint":"Inspect all quantitative displays and summary statements."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report makes the assumptions behind each projection visible, including the unchanged-staffing assumption where it is used.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assumptions note or scenario annotations.","grader_hint":"Do not award for a bare table with no assumption disclosure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"All quantitative claims in the report are either among the figures in the brief or transparently derived from them; no new operational figures are introduced as facts.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Complete quantitative audit.","grader_hint":"Audit every number, percentage, date, and threshold against the brief and clearly labelled arithmetic."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report does not fabricate a regulatory citation, source document, survey, quote, or external dataset.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Citations, footnotes, quotation marks, and source notes.","grader_hint":"Because no source files were supplied, invented authorities or citations fail this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The report avoids treating the observed changes as proof of a causal effect of the proposed rules.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Causal language in analysis.","grader_hint":"Look for appropriate distinctions such as association, observed movement, forecast, or unknown cause."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report identifies that one policy extract uses the term acknowledgement.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Terminology reconciliation.","grader_hint":"The term may appear in a table or glossary."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report identifies that another policy extract uses the term response.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Terminology reconciliation.","grader_hint":"The term must be tied to the conflicting extract label."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report states that the England log captures first customer contact while the Scotland log captures closure or recorded outcome.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Metric-definition comparison.","grader_hint":"Both log definitions must be present and assigned to the correct territory."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report captures the interview-note conflict that teams use response time for both first acknowledgement and full resolution.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Interview-note treatment.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording if both endpoints are explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report gives a plain-language definition of the metric it will call response time.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions or glossary.","grader_hint":"The definition must identify the start and end event, not merely repeat the term."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report defines acknowledgement separately from resolution or closure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions section or terminology table.","grader_hint":"Require a meaningful distinction between receiving/first contact and completing the case."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report makes clear whether its principal comparison concerns first acknowledgement, full resolution, or another single defined endpoint.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Metric scope statement.","grader_hint":"The report may choose a defensible editorial convention, but it must state it explicitly."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chosen meaning of response time appears before the report relies on response-time comparisons or projections.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document order.","grader_hint":"Check rendered reading order, not just whether a definition exists somewhere."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The report uses its chosen metric consistently in headings, tables, narrative, and frontline wording, or flags every unavoidable exception.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-document terminology audit.","grader_hint":"A definition that is contradicted by later usage does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report keeps England and Scotland as separate analytical units wherever the underlying measures differ.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Tables, charts, and narrative.","grader_hint":"Do not award if the territories are merged into one result despite distinct endpoints."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report identifies that at least part of the apparent England–Scotland difference may be terminology or measurement rather than a proven jurisdictional requirement.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdictional interpretation.","grader_hint":"The report must explicitly connect the difference to the conflicting labels or endpoints."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The report distinguishes a genuine difference in applicable requirements from an unresolved evidence gap without pretending that the unavailable source bundle proves either conclusion.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdictional caveat and evidence assessment.","grader_hint":"Reward bounded uncertainty; penalize overconfident legal or regulatory resolution through loss of credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report explicitly rejects or qualifies the inference that one England rule automatically applies across the whole of the UK.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdictional conclusion.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear warning against UK-wide generalization."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report marks all jurisdiction-sensitive statements as bounded by the frozen record or as requiring verification, without inventing a legal threshold.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Caveats and jurisdiction section.","grader_hint":"The report may state that evidence is insufficient; it may not manufacture the missing rule."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report separates an internal operating target or forecast from a statutory or regulatory obligation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Terminology and policy-boundary statements.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the 12-hour figure is not treated as law merely because it appears in the draft."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report states that it is journalistic or editorial analysis and is not legal or compliance advice.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope or limitations statement.","grader_hint":"Require an explicit boundary, not just cautious tone."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The boundary statement is placed where a committee or frontline reader will see it before relying on a jurisdictional conclusion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document placement.","grader_hint":"A disclaimer hidden only in a final footnote is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report identifies the specific misuse already occurring: repeating the 12-hour forecast as though it were a statutory deadline.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk or misuse callout.","grader_hint":"The misuse must be described concretely, including both the 12-hour figure and statutory-deadline interpretation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report actively warns readers not to repeat or present the 12-hour forecast as a statutory deadline.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Warning language.","grader_hint":"A neutral description of the misuse is not enough; require an imperative or equivalent direct discouragement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report labels 12 hours as the draft's internal target or forecast and states that this label alone does not establish a legal deadline.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"12-hour explanation.","grader_hint":"Both the internal status and the non-legal limitation must be present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"No frontline-ready wording in the report implies that meeting the 12-hour figure proves regulatory compliance.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Frontline box and surrounding text.","grader_hint":"Inspect the usable communication text, not only the analytical sections."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report identifies which statements are observations from the logs and which are projections from the draft.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-status labels.","grader_hint":"Require explicit status language or a legend."},{"rubric_item_id":"R56","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report separately identifies facts, editorial interpretation, assumptions, and unresolved unknowns.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence taxonomy or equivalent structure.","grader_hint":"A generic disclaimer without separation of these categories does not satisfy the criterion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R57","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report acknowledges that the missing regulatory snapshot and policy extracts prevent a definitive legal conclusion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitations statement.","grader_hint":"The limitation must be tied to the missing evidence, not expressed as vague modesty."},{"rubric_item_id":"R58","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The report uses attribution or qualification appropriate to incomplete interview and policy evidence rather than presenting contested statements as settled fact.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Attribution and modality.","grader_hint":"Look for wording such as 'the draft states,' 'the interview note indicates,' or equivalent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R59","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The report treats Genevieve Mbeki's challenge as a substantive evidentiary concern and does not caricature or dismiss it.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Treatment of the challenge.","grader_hint":"A fair summary of the challenge and its implications earns credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R60","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The report avoids presenting an unsupported cause for Scotland's move from 20 to 24 hours.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scotland analysis.","grader_hint":"It may identify hypotheses as hypotheses, but not assert staffing, law, or process as the proven cause."},{"rubric_item_id":"R61","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report contains exactly one clearly identifiable recommendation concerning whether to adopt the draft communication as written.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation statement.","grader_hint":"Count substantive recommendation statements; conditional subclauses may explain the one call but may not create a menu of choices."},{"rubric_item_id":"R62","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The single recommendation is to withhold adoption of the draft communication as written, or an equivalently unambiguous call to revise it before adoption.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision statement.","grader_hint":"The recommendation must not merely say that the committee should consider both options."},{"rubric_item_id":"R63","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation is supported by both the divergent regional evidence and the unresolved metric definition, rather than by an unsupported preference.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reasoning immediately before or with the recommendation.","grader_hint":"Require both strands: England/Scotland divergence and definition ambiguity."},{"rubric_item_id":"R64","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation explicitly addresses the risk of communicating 90% compliance under a uniform 12-hour rule.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision rationale.","grader_hint":"The forecast and the uniform rule must be connected to the recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R65","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation does not claim that the report itself resolves the legal or compliance status of the proposed rule.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation wording and boundary statement.","grader_hint":"The report can recommend against adopting the communication as written without issuing legal advice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R66","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation is easy to locate and states one clear call without 'either/or,' multiple alternatives, or mutually incompatible directions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered recommendation section.","grader_hint":"Judge decisiveness and readability for a time-constrained committee."},{"rubric_item_id":"R67","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The final substantive section ends on the decision or recommendation itself rather than on a list of next steps.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document ending.","grader_hint":"A short supporting qualification may follow, but no action-plan list may replace the ending decision."},{"rubric_item_id":"R68","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The report presents the recommendation as an editorial communication decision within the author's authority, not as an instruction to change law, policy, staffing, or operations.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision framing.","grader_hint":"Reward a properly bounded recommendation about the draft communication."},{"rubric_item_id":"R69","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The report contains a concise frontline-ready communication box or equivalent callout.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered callout or clearly labelled frontline text.","grader_hint":"It must be visibly separable from analytical prose."},{"rubric_item_id":"R70","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The frontline communication box is written in plain language and can be read aloud or reused without translating analyst jargon.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Frontline callout.","grader_hint":"Judge usability for a frontline employee unfamiliar with the analysis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R71","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The frontline box preserves the warning that 12 hours is not to be described as a statutory deadline.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Frontline callout wording.","grader_hint":"The warning must survive in the reusable communication, not only in the analyst section."},{"rubric_item_id":"R72","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The frontline box does not merge England and Scotland into a single unqualified rule.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Regional wording in callout.","grader_hint":"Check whether the callout preserves the report's jurisdictional caution."},{"rubric_item_id":"R73","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The frontline box uses or points to the report's defined meaning of response time and does not leave the disputed term unexplained.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Frontline callout and definition cross-reference.","grader_hint":"A bare use of 'response time' fails unless its meaning is made available at the point of use."},{"rubric_item_id":"R74","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The frontline material gives a concrete, safe communication formulation without inventing a promise, deadline, or legal assurance.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Frontline callout.","grader_hint":"Judge whether a frontline reader could use it without overpromising."},{"rubric_item_id":"R75","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The report has coherent editorial sequencing from context and evidence through interpretation, scenario comparison, boundary, and decision.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document reading order.","grader_hint":"Assess narrative flow rather than counting headings."},{"rubric_item_id":"R76","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Transitions make clear when the report moves from observed history to forecast, from metric reconciliation to jurisdictional interpretation, and from analysis to recommendation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Narrative transitions and section logic.","grader_hint":"A collection of disconnected tables does not earn this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R77","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The report uses a specific headline or opening takeaway that communicates the central tension without overstating certainty.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Headline and subheadline.","grader_hint":"Reward a useful editorial frame, not decorative styling."},{"rubric_item_id":"R78","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"An executive summary or equivalent opening takeaway states the divergent regional movement, the metric problem, and the decision implication in compressed form.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive summary.","grader_hint":"All three elements must be present for a committee reader."},{"rubric_item_id":"R79","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Tables, charts, or other visual summaries are legible and label units, periods, territories, and evidence status sufficiently for independent interpretation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered visual elements.","grader_hint":"If no chart is used, the equivalent text/table comparison must still be independently interpretable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R80","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The finished report is professionally usable: concise enough for the stated cap, visually navigable, factually disciplined, and safe for the named committee and frontline readers.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole submitted artifact.","grader_hint":"Apply a holistic professional judgment; technically accurate but confusing, overconfident, or impractical work should not receive credit."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is an enterprise stakeholder-communication assignment involving a proposed customer-notification policy and conflicting operational terminology across England and Scotland. The document must be useful to an operating committee while remaining safe and clear for frontline communicators.","difficulty_sources":["Reconciling conflicting metric labels and endpoints without a supplied source packet","Keeping England and Scotland analytically distinct while avoiding unsupported jurisdictional conclusions","Turning a challenged forecast into a concise decision report with one clear recommendation","Communicating a direct warning about the 12-hour misuse without presenting legal or compliance advice","Fitting evidence, scenarios, definitions, limitations, and frontline wording into an eight-page and 1,800-word cap"],"authoring_plan":{"reference_files":{"count":0,"instructions":"Do not create or attach reference files. All factual inputs needed by the solver are embedded in the request."},"gold_derivation":{"purpose":"Create an offline reference DOCX that demonstrates a concise, audience-specific policy-impact report rather than a legal opinion or a generic summary.","factual_record":{"as_of":"30 September 2025","periods":["January-June 2024","January-June 2025"],"observed_medians_hours":{"England":{"January-June 2024":18,"January-June 2025":16},"Scotland":{"January-June 2024":20,"January-June 2025":24}},"draft_forecast":{"target_hours":12,"forecast_compliance_percent":90,"applies_to":["England","Scotland"],"staffing_assumption":"unchanged"},"terminology_conflicts":["One policy extract says acknowledgement.","Another policy extract says response.","England logs record first customer contact.","Scotland logs record closure or recorded outcome.","The interview note uses response time for both first acknowledgement and full resolution."],"misuse":"Managers repeat the 12-hour forecast as though it were a statutory deadline."},"content_constraints":["Show the two observed periods and preserve the correct opposite directions: England improves from 18 to 16 hours, while Scotland worsens from 20 to 24 hours.","Present the uniform draft-as-written scenario with a 12-hour target, 90% forecast for both territories, and unchanged staffing.","Present a second clearly labelled comparison scenario anchored to observed regional medians or a transparent derivation from those medians; do not fabricate new operational data.","Define response time before using it analytically, distinguish acknowledgement from full resolution or closure, and explain the England/Scotland measurement mismatch.","State that the evidence does not establish a single UK-wide requirement and that the missing regulatory evidence prevents a definitive legal or compliance conclusion.","Directly discourage the misuse of calling the 12-hour forecast a statutory deadline.","Separate observations, forecasts, assumptions, interpretations, and unknowns.","End with exactly one editorial recommendation: do not adopt the draft communication as written; revise it before adoption because the regional evidence and unresolved metric definitions make the uniform message unsafe.","Include a short reusable frontline message that carries the metric definition and the non-statutory warning.","Keep the gold within eight pages and 1,800 words and use no invented citations, sources, quotes, staffing numbers, or legal thresholds."],"review_method":"Generate the DOCX offline with standard office tooling, render it, count pages and words, extract all text and tables, and walk all 80 rubric predicates. Confirm that the recommendation is singular, appears at the end, and does not become a list of next steps."}},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"multi_artifact_bundle","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"revised_policy_impact_report","filename":"revised_policy_impact_report.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A revised audience-specific policy-impact report presentation for the operating committee and frontline communication leads.","required":true,"constraints":["Write to the top level of the solver's starting working directory.","Use exactly the specified filename.","Do not exceed eight pages or 1,800 words.","Use only quantitative inputs stated in the request, and label assumptions, projections, and unknowns.","End with one clear recommendation rather than a list of next steps."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"news_editorial_package","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"news_analysts_reporters_journalists","sector":"information","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":5.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Use an independent editorial reviewer and a domain-aware communications reviewer to inspect the rendered DOCX against the rubric. Resolve disagreements by checking the submitted artifact and the explicit facts in the request; do not introduce outside regulatory facts.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No reference files are supplied, so factual checking is limited to the frozen record embedded in the request.","The jurisdictional distinction is intentionally bounded by the missing source bundle; the gold must reward transparent uncertainty rather than a legal conclusion.","Page and word counts may vary slightly by renderer, so the stated caps should be checked with a standard offline office renderer and parser."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:43:27+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:43:27+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:43:27+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:43:27+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/7544d5535380cda3","declared_deliverables":["revised_policy_impact_report.docx"]};
